# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REFERENCE SOLUTION — do not open this before step 5.
#
# The .EXPECTED extension keeps Cucumber from picking this file up (the engine only
# discovers *.feature). It is here so that after you have written and passed YOUR
# pricing.feature you can diff the two:
#
#   diff -u src/test/resources/features/pricing.feature \
#           src/test/resources/features/pricing.feature.EXPECTED
#
# Differences are not failures. What matters is: did you cover every ruling, is each
# scenario about business behaviour rather than about your API, and did you find any
# example that this file misses?
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Feature: Checkout pricing

  Tiered discounts, promo codes and loyalty points, as agreed in the example-mapping
  session. Every scenario below is a decision that session made — the rulings live in
  docs/example-mapping.md, and each Rule block names the rule card it came from.

  Rule: The tier discount applies only to orders strictly over €100

    Example: An order of exactly €100 misses the discount
      Given a member with 0 points
      When they check out an order of €100.00
      Then the discount is €0.00
      And the amount due is €100.00

    Example: One cent over the threshold earns the discount
      Given a member with 0 points
      When they check out an order of €100.01
      Then the discount is €10.00
      And the amount due is €90.01

    Example: A guest gets no tier discount at any size
      Given a guest with 0 points
      When they check out an order of €250.00
      Then the discount is €0.00
      And the amount due is €250.00

  Rule: Percentage discounts round half up to the nearest cent

    Example: 10% of €107.55 is €10.755, charged as €10.76
      Given a member with 0 points
      When they check out an order of €107.55
      Then the discount is €10.76
      And the amount due is €96.79

    Example: 20% of €149.99 is €29.998, charged as €30.00
      Given a gold member with 0 points
      When they check out an order of €149.99
      Then the discount is €30.00
      And the amount due is €119.99

  Rule: Percentage discounts never stack — the customer gets the better one

    Scenario Outline: Tier discount versus promo code
      Given a <tier> with 0 points
      When they check out an order of €<subtotal> with promo code "<code>"
      Then the discount is €<discount>

      Examples:
        | tier        | subtotal | code      | discount |
        | gold member | 200.00   | WELCOME15 | 40.00    |
        | member      | 200.00   | WELCOME15 | 30.00    |
        | member      | 80.00    | WELCOME15 | 12.00    |
        | guest       | 80.00    | SPRING5   | 4.00     |

  Rule: Points pay for what is left after the discount, in whole 100-point blocks

    Example: A gold member spends 500 points on a discounted order
      Given a gold member with 500 points
      When they check out an order of €200.00 paying with 500 points
      Then the discount is €40.00
      And the points credit is €50.00
      And the amount due is €110.00
      And they earn 11 points
      And their point balance is 11

    Example: Redemption stops at the amount due and the rest stays in the balance
      Given a member with 2000 points
      When they check out an order of €120.00 paying with 2000 points
      Then the points credit is €100.00
      And the amount due is €8.00
      And they earn 0 points
      And their point balance is 1000

    Example: Points come in blocks of 100 or not at all
      Given a member with 500 points
      When they try to check out an order of €120.00 paying with 150 points
      Then checkout is rejected with "points must be redeemed in blocks of 100"

    Example: A guest has no points to spend
      Given a guest with 0 points
      When they try to check out an order of €120.00 paying with 100 points
      Then checkout is rejected with "only members can redeem points"

    Example: A member cannot spend points they do not have
      Given a member with 300 points
      When they try to check out an order of €120.00 paying with 400 points
      Then checkout is rejected with "not enough points"

  Rule: Points are earned on the cash actually paid, one per whole €10

    Example: Earning follows the discounted price, not the ticket price
      Given a gold member with 0 points
      When they check out an order of €200.00
      Then the amount due is €160.00
      And they earn 16 points

    Example: A guest earns nothing
      Given a guest with 0 points
      When they check out an order of €200.00
      Then they earn 0 points
